## Introduction

Tiling mistakes are unusually honest — every one of them ends up visible at eye level, every day, for the life of the installation. The lippage you feel underfoot, the hollow tile that sounds different when tapped, the grout that cracks in straight lines along the joints: all were preventable at a specific earlier moment.

This guide walks the most common tiling failures — substrate shortcuts, layout errors, adhesive coverage gaps, spacing and alignment mistakes, and grouting too early — with the mechanism of each and the specific habit that prevents it. None of the fixes require talent; they require knowing the sequence and respecting the waiting periods.

## Mistake 1: Tiling Onto an Unprepared Substrate

**What it looks like later.** Hollow-sounding tiles within the first year, then tiles that rock and crack at edges, then a floor that fails in patches rather than all at once.

**The mechanism.** Adhesive bonds to the surface it is spread on. A dusty screed, a slab with loose laitance, an old floor with a film of grease, or a fresh screed still drying out gives the adhesive nothing permanent to hold. The tile is bonded — to a layer that is itself bonded to nothing. Add traffic and thermal cycling, and that layer lets go.

**The habits that prevent it.** Test the substrate before anything else: scratch the surface — if it dusts or crumbles, it needs treating; tap across the old floor for hollow areas before tiling over it, because new adhesive faithfully reproduces old debonding. Screeds need their full cure (a week minimum for standard sand-and-cement) and must be dry, level, and swept — then primed if the adhesive's data sheet calls for it. The hour of preparation is the cheapest hour on the job; the re-laying of a failed floor is the most expensive.

## Mistake 2: Starting From the Most Visible Wall Instead of the Best Reference

**What it looks like later.** A room that is "almost square" — the grid runs true along one wall and visibly pinches into a wedge at the opposite one. Everyone who enters notices the wall; the wedge is at eye level on the way in.

**The mechanism.** Rooms are never perfectly square, and a grid started parallel to one wall inherits every deviation of that wall across the whole room. The error is invisible in the first row and grows with every subsequent one.

**The habits that prevent it.** Establish the reference before any adhesive is mixed: find the room's centre or the dominant sightline (usually the entry view), strike perpendicular reference lines with the 3-4-5 method rather than trusting a corner, and dry-lay the first rows to see where the cuts land. The aim is a field that is square to itself, with any necessary small adjustments hidden at the least visible edges. Professionals spend 30 minutes on this that amateurs spend three weeks not noticing they skipped.

## Mistake 3: Dot-and-Dab Adhesive Instead of Full Coverage

**What it looks like later.** The tap test tells the story: a hollow ring underfoot, usually in the middle of tiles, along a pattern that traces exactly where the adhesive dabs were. Some tiles ping hollow within months; edges chip because the middle is unsupported.

**The mechanism.** Five dabs of adhesive per tile — the "dot and dab" habit — leaves voids under most of the tile's area. The tile is held in position but not supported across its face, so point loads (a heel, a chair leg, a dropped pot) flex the tile over an unsupported span until it cracks or debonds. Voids also collect water in wet areas and feed moisture to the grout joints from below.

**The habits that prevent it.** Spread adhesive with the correct notched trowel for the tile size — the ribs of a properly notched bed collapse into full coverage when the tile is pressed home and briefly moved a centimre across the bed. Large-format tiles additionally need back-buttering: a thin skim on the tile's back to fill its texture and guarantee the 80%+ floor coverage that large formats require. After laying a few tiles, pry one up and check: the ribs should have flattened into near-total contact. If the adhesive face has skinned over (sat open too long), scrape it off and spread fresh — skinned adhesive bonds to itself, not to the tile.

## Mistake 4: Wrong Spacing, or No Spacers at All

**What it looks like later.** Joints that wander between hairline and 8 mm across the same room; rows that drift because each tile was nudged to "look right" against the last one. With rectified porcelain, tile edges visibly touch at random spots — the beginning of edge chipping.

**The mechanism.** Tiles are not identical: a few tenths of a millimetre of size variance per tile accumulates across a room. Joints absorb that variance. Without consistent spacers, the variance accumulates in the layout instead, and every "small adjustment" compounds.

**The habits that prevent it.** Spacers in every joint, every tile, checked with a straightedge across the field every few rows — a 1 m level laid across three tiles instantly reveals a joint drifting out of line. Choose the joint width per the tile type: 3 mm for rectified porcelain, 4–5 mm for standard ceramic where the extra tolerance hides calibration variance. And keep to one size: the "we ran out of 3s" mid-room switch to 4 mm is visible forever at exactly the row where it happened.

## Mistake 5: Grouting Before the Adhesive Has Cured

**What it looks like later.** Grout that stays permanently dark in patches (moisture trapped below), grout that cracks as tiles micro-shift under traffic before the adhesive is fully anchored, and occasionally a corner of grout that never properly hardens at all.

**The mechanism.** Adhesive cures by both chemical set and drying. Grouting on the same day seals the drying path — the adhesive under the tiles stays wet longer, cures weaker, and the tiles it holds can still move slightly while the grout above them is rigid and set. The grout then does the moving by cracking.

**The habits that prevent it.** Respect the adhesive's stated cure before grouting — 24 hours is the common floor minimum, 48 for cool or humid conditions. The day between laying and grouting is not idle time; it is when the floor becomes a floor. Use it to plan the grout colour sample and check the layout with the straightedge one last time while corrections are still cheap.

## Mistake 6: Not Mixing Cartons — and Not Reading Shade Codes

**What it looks like later.** A floor with a subtle but unmistakable band or patch — one section of the room a half-tone different from the rest, tracing the boundary of the second batch of tiles bought from a different production run.

**The mechanism.** Tiles are fired in batches, and shade and calibre vary slightly between them. Two batches that match at a glance differ visibly under room lighting across a full floor. The calibre code (size class) matters too: two "400 mm" tiles from different calibres are a fraction of a millimetre different — enough to defeat consistent spacing.

**The habits that prevent it.** Before laying, check that every carton carries the same shade and calibre code on its label; if buying in two visits, match the codes exactly or accept the difference knowingly, in writing. Then mix tiles from several cartons as you lay rather than emptying one carton at a time — this spreads any residual variance into a gradual texture instead of a line. It costs nothing and is the single cheapest guarantee against the most embarrassing visible failure in tiling.

## Mistake 7: Cutting Carelessly — or Measuring From the Drawing Instead of the Wall

**What it looks like later.** Perimeter cuts of visibly different widths that should have matched; a door threshold with a jagged edge; two tiles meeting at an internal corner with a growing wedge of grout.

**The mechanism.** Cut tiles are laid against walls that are not straight, so measuring each cut from the actual gap — not from the plan's nominal dimension — is the only reliable method. Careless cutting adds its own defects: unbacked tiles snapping at the last second, angle-grinder edges left rough, and the classic "cut on the wrong side" tile with its factory edge facing the wall and its rough cut in the joint.

**The habits that prevent it.** Measure each perimeter tile in place: hold the last full tile, mark the gap to the wall, subtract the joint allowance twice (joint at the field side and at the wall side). Cut with the tile fully supported — a score-and-snap on a flat surface for straight cuts, an angle grinder with a diamond blade for notches, drilling before cutting for pipe holes so the tile doesn't crack through the hole. Dry-fit every cut before buttering it; the five seconds of placing it beats the five minutes of prying up a glued wrong cut. And remember which edge is which: the factory edge meets the joint; the cut edge meets the wall.

## Mistake 8: Ignoring Movement — No Perimeter Gaps, No Soft Joints

**What it looks like later.** Tiles that "pop" audibly in their first hot season — typically along a straight line parallel to a wall, at the exact distance where the floor meets a restrained edge. A tile tented off the floor as a single rigid sheet is the extreme version.

**The mechanism.** Every floor expands with heat and gains dimension with moisture. Tiled floors want to move as a sheet, and they need a free edge to move toward. If the tiles run hard against walls, thresholds, and columns with no compressible gap, the expansion has nowhere to go — so it goes up, debonding the field along the line of greatest stress.

**The habits that prevent it.** Leave a perimeter gap of 6–10 mm at every restraining edge — it disappears under the skirting and is the cheapest insurance in the whole trade. In large rooms, break the field with a movement joint (a flexible profile strip) every 5–8 metres and at doorways; in wet areas, use silicone at wall-floor junctions rather than grout, per the detailing in [our waterproofing guide](/learn/how-to-tile-bathroom-waterproofing-best-practices). Tile work is rigid by design; the building is not, and the joints are where that difference is reconciled.

## Mistake 9: Wrong Adhesive for the Tile or the Location

**What it looks like later.** Porcelain tiles that debond despite good coverage — the adhesive cured hard but never gripped the dense body; outdoor floors that fail after the first wet season; glass or stone tiles permanently shaded by adhesive showing through.

**The mechanism.** Adhesive chemistry is matched to substrate and tile. Standard cement adhesive grips porous ceramic but struggles to bond dense porcelain, which needs a porcelain-rated (C2-class) adhesive. Exterior and wet-area work needs flexibility and water resistance grades that interior wall adhesive does not have. Natural stone, being translucent at thin thicknesses, shows the adhesive colour through the tile face — grey adhesive under light travertine turns it permanently dull.

**The habits that prevent it.** Buy adhesive by specification, not by price per bag: check the tile type (ceramic/porcelain/stone/glass) and location (dry interior/wet/exterior) against the adhesive's stated use on the bag, and when in doubt, buy the flexible porcelain grade — it works everywhere standard does, with margin. For light stone, use white adhesive. The data-sheet minute costs nothing; the wrong-adhesive failure costs the entire floor.

## Mistake 10: Paying Before the Final Inspection

**What it looks like later.** The tiler is gone, and the defects list is yours to fund: the hollow tile at the doorway, the lippage underfoot at the room's centre, the grout haze film over half the floor.

**The mechanism.** Every defect in this guide is cheap to fix while the tiler is on site with materials in hand — and expensive once they are not. The final inspection is the moment the leverage changes hands.

**The habits that prevent it.** Walk the floor with the tiler before final payment, with a straightedge, a torch, and the tap of a knuckle or coin: every tile sounds solid (no hollow pings), a straightedge across joints shows minimal lippage (a coin shouldn't catch on the edge joints), cuts match in visible places, grout is full and clean, and perimeter gaps are left free, not grouted solid. Agree the punch list, hold a retention until it is done, and do the silicone joints after the grout has cured — the sequence matters as much as the inspection.

## The Sequence That Prevents All of Them

Every mistake above has a prevention that is a habit, and the habits stack into one sequence. Done in order, each step removes the failure mode of the step before it:

1. Prepare and test the substrate; cure, sweep, prime
2. Dry-lay the field and strike true reference lines (3-4-5)
3. Check shade and calibre codes; mix cartons as you lay
4. Correct adhesive for tile and location; notched bed, back-butter large formats
5. Spacers in every joint; straightedge across the field every few rows
6. Measure each perimeter cut from the actual gap; dry-fit before buttering
7. Cure the adhesive fully before grouting (24–48 h)
8. Grout to the mixing and packing rules; two-bucket clean ([grout guide here](/learn/tile-grout-selection-and-application))
9. Perimeter gaps and movement joints; silicone at junctions, not grout
10. Final inspection with the tiler on site — then pay

## Conclusion

Tiling fails in the same ten ways, at the same ten moments, and every failure was a skipped preparation rather than a lack of skill. The sequence above takes barely longer than improvisation — preparation and set-out happen before the clock feels like it is running — and its result is a floor that sounds solid, feels flat, and stays that way through hot seasons and wet ones.

If you are planning the quantities and costs for a job you now intend to do properly, continue with [our tile quantity calculation guide](/learn/how-to-calculate-tile-quantities-any-room) and the [tile cost estimation guide](/learn/tile-cost-estimation-materials-labour-budget) — a floor measured, budgeted, and executed by this sequence is a floor you lay once.
